# ---------- F2 Valid? Sound? (L10)
("F2",10,"Premise 1: All psychologists study behaviour. Premise 2: Lina is a psychologist. Conclusion: Lina studies behaviour. This argument is…",
 ["…valid and sound.","…valid but unsound.","…invalid and unsound.","…invalid but sound."],
 "If the premises are true, the conclusion must be true (valid), and the premises are true (sound)."),
("F2",10,"Premise 1: All cats can fly. Premise 2: Milo is a cat. Conclusion: Milo can fly. This argument is…",
 ["…valid but unsound.","…valid and sound.","…invalid and unsound.","…invalid but sound."],
 "The structure is correct (valid), but Premise 1 is false, so the argument is unsound."),
("F2",10,"Premise 1: If it rains, the field gets wet. Premise 2: The field is wet. Conclusion: It rained. This argument is…",
 ["…invalid and unsound.","…valid and sound.","…valid but unsound.","…sound but invalid."],
 "It affirms the consequent: the field could be wet for other reasons (sprinklers), so it is invalid and therefore automatically unsound."),
("F2",10,"Which statement about deductive arguments is correct?",
 ["An invalid argument is automatically unsound.","A sound argument can be invalid.","Validity depends on whether the premises are actually true.","A valid argument always has a true conclusion."],
 "Soundness requires validity plus true premises, so an invalid argument can never be sound. Validity is about structure only."),
